Fictional EP Tracklist

Track 1: Dead Letters Shiver in the Mailbag
- Story & Context: Inspector Julian Karr uncovered three water-damaged mail sacks inside the frozen hull of a beached coastal cutter off Cape Disappointment, the damp cedar and rancid wax seals giving up decades of unsent correspondence. Dahl translated the irregular, decelerating heartbeat of a sea otter trapped beneath shoreline ice into her pedal-hat pacing while Vance tracked his muted horn in a single unedited take, the band recording as the studio heating system failed completely. The song pivots on the realization that every letter was addressed to people who had perished during the maritime freeze of 1947, culminating in Vance’s hushed vocal murmur: "The ink stayed wet while the blood froze solid."
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Cinematic macro view of an antique, frost-encrusted envelope sealed with dark crimson wax resting on a salt-bleached wooden floor, icy condensation beading across handwritten cursive, cold atmospheric monochrome jazz record aesthetic.

Track 2: Why Write If the Ground Is Frozen?
- Story & Context: In the windowless vault of an Astoria dead-letter facility, bassist Vesper Lind found a unsent telegram from her estranged father that had been misfiled for thirty years, its brittle paper smelling of sulfur and wet spruce. During the tracking session, Lind insisted on detuning her gut strings to an open sub-C and played only four notes per minute, perfectly syncing her rhythm to the metabolic torpor of an arctic ground squirrel curled in subterranean winter sleep. Moreau's baritone sax weeps counterpoint against Dahl’s solitary hi-hat clicks, framing Lind’s low, spoken confession: "I kept the stamp and drowned your name in frost."
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: A solitary woman in a heavy black mourning coat standing under an iron streetlamp in a desolate, snow-covered coastal port, holding an unread letter as salt-fog rolls around her boots, cinematic film noir, grainy 1960s aesthetic.

Track 3: Flutter Bias on the Autopsy Tape
- Story & Context: During a blizzard that knocked out power across the Tillamook coast, the band ran their master reel-to-reel deck on a failing gas generator, causing the tape speed to drift and drop into deep, mechanical swoons. The surviving track captures the physical tape scraping against the capstan, creating a low, sinister shudder that biologists in the scene noted perfectly duplicated the bradycardia of a denning polar bear. As the generator choked on frozen fuel and died mid-reverb tail, Vance blew one final, breathless tone into the bell of his horn, whispering the track's closing eulogy: "Nothing warms under the needle except the regret."
